{"id":"m1-l2","title":"Introduction to Verilog","aspect":0.5625,"wpt":960,"slides":[{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Introduction to Verilog","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.2662,0.8625,0.6345],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Introduction to CAD Tools","b":1,"sz":28.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"To design a logic circuit, a number of CAD tools are needed.","sz":28.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Usually packaged together into a CAD system, which typically includes tools for the following tasks: design entry, synthesis and optimization, simulation, and physical design.","sz":28.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Design Entry","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0534,0.3576,0.8864,0.6202],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"The starting point in the process of designing a logic circuit is the conception of what the circuit is supposed to do and the formulation of its general structure. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The first stage of this process involves entering into the CAD system a ","sz":24.0},{"text":"description of the circuit ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"being designed. This stage is called design entry.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"For design entry we are ","sz":24.0},{"text":"writing source code ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"in a ","sz":24.0},{"text":"hardware description language","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":".","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Hardware Description Languages","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0947,0.3796,0.878,0.4981],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"A hardware description language (HDL) is similar to a typical computer programming language except that an HDL is used to describe hardware rather than a program to be executed on a computer. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Two HDLs are IEEE standards: Verilog HDL and VHDL.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Why to use Verilog","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.059,0.2465,0.861,0.4981],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Supported by most companies that offer digital hardware technology. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Verilog provides ","sz":24.0},{"text":"design portability","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":". A circuit specified in Verilog can be implemented in different types of chips and with CAD tools provided by different companies, without having to change the Verilog specification.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Both small and large logic circuit designs can be efficiently represented in Verilog code.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Representation of Digital Circuits in Verilog","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0712,0.213,0.8575,0.4981],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"i","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":") Structural representation- ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"A larger circuit is defined by writing code that connects simple circuit elements together.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just"},{"runs":[{"text":"Using Verilog constructs, describe the structure of the circuit in terms of circuit elements, such as logic gates. A larger circuit is defined by writing code that connects such elements together. This approach is referred to as the ","sz":24.0},{"text":"structural ","sz":24.0,"i":1},{"text":"representation of logic circuits. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just"},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"ii) ","sz":24.0},{"text":"Behavioral representation- ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"Describing a circuit by using logic expressions and programming constructs that define the behavior of the circuit but not its actual structure in terms of gates.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just"},{"runs":[{"text":"Using logic expressions and Verilog programming constructs that define the desired behavior of the circuit, but not its actual structure in terms of gates, describe a circuit more abstractly.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just"}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Structural Specification of Logic Circuits","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.2716,0.8602,0.5818],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Verilog includes a set of gate-level primitives that correspond to commonly-used logic gates.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"A gate is represented by indicating its functional name, output, and inputs. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"For example,","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"A two-input AND gate, with inputs x1 and x2 and output y, is denoted as","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"      and ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"(y, x1, x2);","sz":24.0}],"align":"just"},{"runs":[{"text":"A four-input OR gate is specified as","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"       or ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"(y, x1, x2, x3, x4);","sz":24.0}],"align":"just"},{"runs":[{"text":"The NOT gate given by ","sz":24.0},{"text":"not ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"(y, x); implements y = x\u2019. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"img","box":[0.1578,0.1351,0.6683,0.8142],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/1.webp"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0656,0.0432,0.71,0.0853],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"The available Verilog gate-level primitives are  ","sz":32.0}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0292,0.083,0.9131,0.6343],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Verilog Module","sz":24.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[{"text":"It is a circuit or sub circuit described with Verilog code. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The module has a name, ","sz":24.0},{"text":"module_name","sz":24.0,"b":1,"i":1},{"text":", which can be any valid identifier, followed by a list of ports. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The term port refers to an input or output connection in an electrical circuit. The ports can be of type ","sz":24.0},{"text":"input","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":", ","sz":24.0},{"text":"output","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":", or ","sz":24.0},{"text":"inout","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"(bidirectional), and can be either scalar or vector","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The General Form of a Module","sz":24.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[{"text":"module ","b":1,"sz":24.0},{"text":"module","sz":24.0},{"text":" name [(port name{, port name})]; ","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1},{"runs":[{"text":"[","sz":24.0},{"text":"parameter ","b":1,"sz":24.0},{"text":"declarations]","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1},{"runs":[{"text":"[","sz":24.0},{"text":"input ","b":1,"sz":24.0},{"text":"declarations]","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1},{"runs":[{"text":"[","sz":24.0},{"text":"output ","b":1,"sz":24.0},{"text":"declarations]","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1},{"runs":[{"text":"[","sz":24.0},{"text":"inout","b":1,"sz":24.0},{"text":" ","b":1,"sz":24.0},{"text":"declarations]","sz":24.0}],"lvl":1},{"runs":[{"text":"endmodule","b":1,"sz":24.0}],"lvl":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.071,0.134,0.4014,0.069],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Identifier Names","b":1,"sz":36.0},{"text":"\n","sz":36.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0491,0.1899,0.8888,0.6202],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Identifiers are the names of variables and other elements in Verilog code. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The rules for specifying identifiers are simple: any letter or digit may be used, as well as the _ underscore and $ characters. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"An identifier must not begin with a digit and it should not be a Verilog keyword. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Examples of legal identifiers are f, x1, x, y, and Byte.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Some examples of illegal names are 1x, +y, x*y, and 258 ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Verilog is case sensitive, hence k is not the same as K, and BYTE is not the same as Byte.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"\u00a0Verilog Operators","sz":24.0,"b":1}],"align":"just"},{"runs":[{"text":"Verilog operators are useful for synthesizing logic circuits.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Documentation in Verilog Code","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0947,0.3796,0.8507,0.5476],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Documentation can be included in Verilog code by writing a comment. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"A short comment begins with the double slash, //, and continues to the end of the line. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"A long comment can span multiple lines and is contained inside the delimiters /* and */.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"White Space","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0608,0.2939,0.8507,0.4981],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"White space characters, such as SPACE and TAB, and blank lines are ignored by the Verilog compiler. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Multiple statements can be written on a single line. ","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Placing each statement on a separate line and using indentation within blocks of code, such as an ","sz":24.0},{"text":"if-else ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"statement are good ways to increase the readability of code.","sz":24.0}],"align":"just","bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.6432,0.148],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Gate level primitives(structural)","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.2662,0.425,0.6345],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Simple logic function","sz":28.0}],"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.5092,0.3796,0.4544,0.5901],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Using gate level primitives","sz":20.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"module example2(x1,x2,x3,f);","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tinput x1,x2,x3;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\toutput f;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tand (g,x1,x2);","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tnot (k,x2);","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tand (h,k,x3);","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tor (","sz":20.0},{"text":"f,g,h","sz":20.0},{"text":");","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"endmodule","sz":20.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.0808,0.3875,0.3367,0.5297],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/2.webp"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1043],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Gate Level Primitives","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.1182,0.1899,0.7591,0.7475],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/3.webp"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Behavioral Specification of Logic Circuits","b":1,"sz":40.0},{"text":"\n","sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0617,0.305,0.82,0.582],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Gate level primitives can be tedious when large circuits have to be designed. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"To use more abstract expressions and programming constructs to describe the behavior of a digital circuit.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"To define the circuit using logic expressions. The AND ","sz":24.0},{"text":"and","sz":24.0},{"text":" OR operations are indicated by the \u201c&\u201d and \u201c|\u201d signs, respectively. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"The ","sz":24.0},{"text":"assign","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" keyword provides a continuous assignment for the output signal. ","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1},{"runs":[{"text":"Whenever any signal on the right-hand side changes its state, the value of output will be re-evaluated.","sz":24.0}],"bullet":1}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0688,0.0532,0.8625,0.1933],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Behavioral(Continuous assignment) Specification of Logic Circuits","b":1,"sz":40.0}],"title":1}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.141,0.4699,0.2805,0.227],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/2.webp"},{"t":"text","box":[0.5062,0.2662,0.425,0.6345],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"module example2 (x1, x2, x3, f);","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tinput x1, x2, x3;","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\toutput f;","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"    assign f = (x1 & x2) | ( ~x2 & x3);","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"endmodule","sz":24.0}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.033,0.1701,0.9411,0.6059],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"1.\tCreate a directory with section followed by roll number (to be unique); e.g. CCE B21","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"2.\tOpen any text editor in Ubuntu (say, ","sz":24.0},{"text":"gedit","sz":24.0},{"text":") and type the source code.","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"module example2(x1, x2, x3, f);","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"input x1, x2, x3;","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"output f;","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"and (g, x1, x2);","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"not (k, x2);","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"and (h, k, x3);","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"or (f, g, h);","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7},{"runs":[{"text":"endmodule","sz":24.0}],"lvl":7}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0545,0.0429,0.5,0.0715],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"Running a sample Verilog code","sz":24.0,"b":1}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0753,0.1681,0.8495,0.3366],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"3. Save the source code with file name same as module name but with \u2018","sz":24.0,"sub":1,"c":"#000000"},{"text":".v\u2019","sz":24.0,"b":1,"sub":1,"c":"#000000"},{"text":" extension in the required directory. ","sz":24.0,"sub":1,"c":"#000000"}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"4. Type the following testbench code.","sz":24.0,"sub":1,"c":"#000000"}]}],"anchor":"ctr"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.1482,0.1046,0.6357,0.8213],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"`","sz":24.0},{"text":"timescale","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" 1ns/1ns","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"`","sz":24.0},{"text":"include","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" \"example2.v\"    //Name of the Verilog file","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"module","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" example2_tb();","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"reg","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" x1, x2, x3;\t\t//Input","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"wire","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" f;\t\t\t//Output","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"example2 ex2(x1, x2, x3, f);\t//Instantiation of the module","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"initial","sz":24.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[{"text":"begin","sz":24.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"\t","sz":24.0},{"text":"$","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"dumpfile","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"(\"example2_tb.vcd\");","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t","sz":24.0},{"text":"$","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"dumpvars","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"(0, example2_tb);","sz":24.0}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.1973,0.0441,0.5,0.9559],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"               x1=1'b0; x2=1'b0; x3=1'b0;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b0; x2=1'b0; x3=1'b1;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b0; x2=1'b1; x3=1'b0;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b0; x2=1'b1; x3=1'b1;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b1; x2=1'b0; x3=1'b0;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b1; x2=1'b0; x3=1'b1;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b1; x2=1'b1; x3=1'b0;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\tx1=1'b1; x2=1'b1; x3=1'b1;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\t#20;","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"\t","sz":20.0},{"text":"$display","sz":20.0,"b":1},{"text":"(\"Test complete\");","sz":20.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"end","sz":20.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"endmodule","sz":20.0,"b":1}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0768,0.232,0.8527,0.2289],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"5.\tSave the source code with file name same as module name but with \u2018.v\u2019 extension in the same directory. ","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"6.\tGo to the terminal. Type the following commands for compilation.","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\u2022\t","sz":24.0},{"text":"iverilog","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" -o example2_tb.vvp example2_tb.v ","sz":24.0,"b":1}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"text","box":[0.0768,0.5245,0.8527,0.3904],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"On successful execution, file example2_tb.vvp is created.","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"\u2022\t","sz":24.0},{"text":"vvp","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" example2_tb.vvp   ","sz":24.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[],"gap":1},{"runs":[{"text":"On successful execution, file example2_tb.vcd is created and the following messages are displayed in the terminal.","sz":24.0}]},{"runs":[{"text":"VCD info: ","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":"dumpfile","sz":24.0,"b":1},{"text":" example2_tb.vcd opened for output.","sz":24.0,"b":1}]},{"runs":[{"text":"Test complete      ","sz":24.0,"b":1}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"img","box":[0.1089,0.1121,0.8388,0.2825],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/4.webp","crop":[0.3676,0.42382,0.15576,0.41551]},{"t":"text","box":[0.1089,0.5252,0.8464,0.175],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"7.\tOpen ","sz":24.0},{"text":"gtkwave","sz":24.0},{"text":" by double clicking the example2_tb.vcd file available in your directory or type ","sz":24.0},{"text":"gtkwave","sz":24.0},{"text":" in the terminal and open the example2_tb.vcd file in ","sz":24.0},{"text":"gtkwave","sz":24.0},{"text":". A window appears as shown below.","sz":24.0}]}],"anchor":"t"}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0911,0.1517,0.7786,0.175],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"8.\tExpand example2_tb that appears in the left pane. The screen appears as shown below. It can be observed that the input and output variables appear in the bottom left pane.","sz":24.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.1437,0.454,0.7036,0.3335],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/5.webp","crop":[0.04203,0.06089,0.0,0.36612]}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.0411,0.1093,0.867,0.175],"paras":[{"runs":[{"text":"9.\tDrag and drop the variables that appears in the Signals tab of the bottom left pane into Signals column that appears in the middle of the window. The output appears as shown below. Cross-verify the output with the truth table.","sz":24.0}]}],"anchor":"t"},{"t":"img","box":[0.0946,0.3952,0.8134,0.4954],"src":"/dsco/slides/m1-l2/6.webp","crop":[0.04305,0.02976,0.33161,0.41754]}]},{"bg":null,"items":[{"t":"text","box":[0.064